Another helpful question is: **What kind of honor does Jesus deserve?**

Jesus says, “The Father judges no one, but has given all judgment to the Son, that all may honor the Son, just as they honor the Father.” — John 5:22–23

If the Father commands us to honor the Son just as we honor the Father, what kind of honor is Jesus worthy of? If we refuse to give Jesus the honor the Father says He deserves, are we truly honoring the Father?

Thomas saw the risen Jesus and said to Him, “My Lord and my God\!” — John 20:28. Jesus did not rebuke Thomas. Instead, He said, “Have you believed because you have seen me? Blessed are those who have not seen and yet have believed.” — John 20:29

Hebrews 1 is also important because the Father distinguishes the Son from the angels. Then Hebrews 1:10–12 says of the Son, “You, Lord, laid the foundation of the earth in the beginning, and the heavens are the work of your hands.” This quotes Psalm 102, which is about Yahweh/Jehovah as the eternal Creator. So why does Hebrews apply that language to the Son if Jesus is merely a created angel?

Revelation also raises a serious question. Revelation 22 says, “Behold, I am coming soon, bringing my recompense with me, to repay each one for what he has done. I am the Alpha and the Omega, the first and the last, the beginning and the end.” The chapter ends, “Amen. Come, Lord Jesus\!” If Jesus is the coming Judge, the First and the Last, and the Alpha and Omega, can He be a created being?

Jehovah’s Witnesses emphasize the Kingdom, which is good. But the question is: **Who is the King?** If Jesus is the King, Judge, Creator, First and Last, and the one who receives the same honor as the Father, then getting Jesus right is not a side issue. It is central.

